From: DockSeed <dockseed@proton.me>
Subject: [PATCH] arm64: dts: a733: idle injection between throttling and shutdown

The thermal design had one actuator. Between the 90 C passive trip and the
110 C critical trip, cpufreq throttling was the only thing standing between a
hot board and a power-off: if dropping to the lowest OPP cannot hold the part,
nothing else is tried.

The vendor does not have that gap, and this stage is taken from its device
tree. The vendor sun60iw2p1.dtsi (linux-6.6 BSP) gives cpu2..cpu7 a
thermal-idle node and declares two idle zones, cpul_idle_zone and
cpub_idle_zone, each with passive trips at 90 and 100 C; on the 100 C trip it
maps cpu2..cpu5 at 0..50 and cpu6..cpu7 at 0..100. A running vendor board
registers idle-cpu2 .. idle-cpu7 next to its cpufreq, fan, NPU, GPU and PCIe
cooling devices. The
100 C trip and the choice of cpu2..cpu7 come from there. What is ours is the
map bounds: lower 50 / upper unbounded on all six CPUs, which is the opposite
of the vendor's upper limit of 50 on the A55s. The reason is measured and
given below; the vendor's bounds are not adopted.

Why it belongs in the default rather than beside the fan: it needs no hardware.
A fan is a property of one board and helps no other; idle injection works on any
A733, and the board that has no cooling at all is exactly the one with nothing
left at 100 C. The datasheet makes that case concrete rather than theoretical --
Ta is specified to 75 C (Table 6-1) and theta-JA is 25.265 C/W typical (Table
6-3), so in a warm enclosure a couple of watts already puts the junction near the
125 C absolute maximum before any load margin exists.

Registration path, read in the source rather than assumed. CONFIG_ARM_PSCI_CPUIDLE
is =y in the arm64 defconfig; psci_idle_init_cpu() runs once per CPU
(cpuidle-psci.c:434) and each call ends in cpuidle_cooling_register()
(cpuidle-psci.c:412), which looks for a "thermal-idle" child of that CPU's node
and registers one cooling device per CPU that has one
(cpuidle_cooling.c:249-260). That is the shape observed on the 6.6 boards, where
the devices appear as idle-cpu2 .. idle-cpu7.

Two Kconfig symbols are needed and neither is on by default: IDLE_INJECT (the
framework, default n) and CPU_IDLE_THERMAL (the cooling device, bool, depends on
IDLE_INJECT). Both are added to A733_CONFIGS, which asserts them after
olddefconfig, so a defconfig change cannot silently drop the stage.

cpu0 and cpu1 deliberately get no node. Injecting idle on every core of the
little cluster starves the system that is trying to recover; two cores stay
uninjectable so userspace keeps running while the rest are parked. The vendor
DT leaves the same two cores out; the reason is stated here rather than
inherited.

duration-us = 10000 is longer than the 3570 us min-residency of both core idle
states, so an injected period is long enough for the deep state to be worth
entering. It is also the worst case by which this stage can delay a softirq --
see the NOHZ note at the end.

exit-latency-us = 500 is a latency budget for the injected state. The
forced-idle path (idle_inject -> play_idle_precise() ->
cpuidle_use_deepest_state()) has no governor: find_deepest_state()
(drivers/cpuidle/cpuidle.c) takes the deepest enabled state whose exit_latency
is within the limit, and that figure is entry + exit
(drivers/cpuidle/dt_idle_states.c:64). `arm64-dts-a733-psci-idle-states` declares one core power-down
state per cluster, with latencies measured on this chain at the lowest OPP:
cpu_sleep_0_a55, 50 + 150 = 200 us, on cpu0-5 and cpu_sleep_0_a76,
50 + 210 = 260 us, on cpu6-7. 500 admits both, the slower one 240 us inside
the limit, so a latency that is remeasured upwards does not silently put the
injection back on WFI. It also bounds the late start of the next run window
at 5 % of the 10 ms injected period. The vendor DT and the binding's example
carry the same value for the same duration; that is noted, not the reason.

A limit of 100 used to keep the injection on WFI on purpose, while the
firmware refused CPU_SUSPEND: a refused state on this path is selected again
by the do_idle() loop until the injection timer fires (kernel/sched/idle.c,
no governor, no fallback), a full-clock spin instead of idle. That hazard
belongs to declaring a state the firmware does not implement. `arm64-dts-a733-psci-idle-states` declares
core power-down only, which the firmware implements (the TF-A patch `allwinner-a733-native-psci-cpu-suspend`).

Measured on the DT before `arm64-dts-a733-psci-idle-states` took per-cluster states: one state,
cpu_sleep_0, 46 + 59 = 105 us on both clusters, with the same PSCI parameter
and firmware path as the two states now; a limit of 500 admits either. Same
kernel and DT except this value, 50 % injection on cpu2..cpu7
set by hand (step_wise leaves a state at the map's lower bound alone while the
trip is not crossed), one stress-ng matrixprod worker pinned per CPU, 240 s per
arm, arms in one boot in the order WFI, deep, deep, WFI. The WFI arms disable
cpu_sleep_0 on the injected CPUs, which find_deepest_state() skips exactly as
it skips a state over the limit; a run on the old limit of 100 matched them.

                              WFI           core power-down
  cpul / cpub, last ~140 s    53.0-53.3 C   51.4 C
                              53.5 C        51.7-51.8 C
  wall power, same window     6.22 W        5.99-6.03 W
  injected idle, s, A55s+A76s 485.9 + 243.3 486.5 + 245.3

For the same injected time both zones run ~1.8 C cooler and the board draws
0.21 W less, 9 % of what the load adds over idle. The throughput cost on the
injected cores is about the size of the spread between runs of the uninjected
ones (+-1.3 %): -1.4 % on the A76s, of which the wake itself explains ~0.6 %
(117 us at the top OPP, where the load ran, once per 20 ms period); the rest
is not attributed. On the A55s it is not resolved.

One injected core per run did not get the deep state: one A55 injected all
of its idle in WFI, and 75-90 % of the injected time was in core power-down.
The leading explanation is the tick broadcast. This DT has no always-on
broadcast timer, so the hrtimer broadcast is in use, and the CPU it is bound to
gets -EBUSY from tick_broadcast_enter() (kernel/time/tick-broadcast.c,
broadcast_needs_cpu()); cpuidle_enter_state() then falls back to the deepest
state without CPUIDLE_FLAG_TIMER_STOP, which is WFI.

One log line this stage will produce, once per boot at most (ratelimit 10 in
report_idle_softirq(), kernel/time/tick-sched.c:1159):

  NOHZ tick-stop error: local softirq work is pending, handler #80!!!

0x80 = BIT(7) = SCHED_SOFTIRQ (include/linux/interrupt.h:547-561). It is a known
upstream consequence of idle injection, not a defect in this patch: the
idle_inject/N kthreads are SCHED_FIFO prio 50 (idle_inject.c:296-299 ->
sched_set_fifo()) while ksoftirqd is SCHED_OTHER, so a softirq punted to
ksoftirqd while injection is running does not set need_resched
(wakeup_preempt(), kernel/sched/core.c:2208-2223), and can_stop_idle_tick()
reaches report_idle_softirq() with a softirq still pending. Frederic Weisbecker
described exactly this in the 2022 "Forced idle and Non-RCU local softirq
pending" RFC, whose fix was never merged -- 6.18.36's play_idle_precise() has no
ksoftirqd check. Cost: one skipped tick-stop, and a softirq delayed by at most
duration-us. Correctness is unaffected; rcutorture's console-badness.sh filters
the message out of its failure detection.

The trip sits at 100 C: above the 90 C throttle so it only engages when
throttling could not hold the part, and 10 C below critical so there is room for
it to act. The 100 C value is the vendor DT's idle trip (its idle zones also
carry a 90 C trip, with no cooling map on it).

Effect measured rather than argued, on a 6.6 board that already carries the
mechanism: under a constant eight-core load, 50 % injection takes the CPU zones
from 74-76 C to 64 C -- and the cpufreq cooling states fall back to 0, i.e. both
clusters return to full clock because the die is cool enough not to need them.
100 % reaches 53-58 C. Its cost was NOT measured (the throughput counter in that
run died in a timeout); ~50 % injection implies roughly half throughput, which is
exactly why the stage sits above the cpufreq trip and not below it.

That measurement is also where the map's lower bound comes from, and the bound is
load-bearing. A cpuidle cooling device is percentile: max_state is a fixed 100
(cpuidle_cooling.c:68-73). With lower left at THERMAL_NO_LIMIT it reads as 0, and
step_wise enters a newly throttled instance at clamp(cur_state + 1, lower, upper)
= 1 (gov_step_wise.c, get_target_state) -- one percent injection, about 0.2 K of
authority, in a band that is 10 K wide. It then climbs one percent per poll and
depends on the trend staying RAISING to do even that. The first bare-test boot
shows exactly this: the die crossed 100 C at t=70 s and 103 C at t=86 s, sixteen
seconds and about thirty-two governor polls, and the injection states reached 4
and 1. The stage was registered, bound, entered and worthless.

lower = 50 fixes it at the one place the trend defect cannot reach: the entry
state is taken on the first poll after the trip is crossed, without traversing
the ladder, so it does not matter how the trend dithers afterwards. 50 is the
measured number above rather than a round one -- it is the injection level shown
to remove the entire cpufreq throttling demand and buy ~10 K, which is the width
of the band between this trip and critical. upper stays unbounded so the ladder
can still climb to 100 if 50 does not hold, and the descent floor becomes
min(lower + 1, upper) = 51 until the trip clears entirely.

Trip and map numbering: trip-2 and map1 are left free for the board fan patch,
which is held back until a fan on J7 has actually been shown to turn, so this
takes trip-3 and map2. The gap in the node names is deliberate and means the fan
patch drops in later without renumbering anything here. Names are names -- what
the thermal core reads is the position in the trip array, and that is unchanged
either way. Reusing a name would not create a second node: dtc merges same-named
nodes and would silently keep one temperature.

Falsifier: /sys/class/thermal gains six idle-cpuN cooling devices; under a load
that cpufreq alone cannot hold, their cur_state leaves 0 before the critical
trip is approached; and while they inject, the usage of cpuidle state1
(cpu-sleep-0-a55 on cpu2..cpu5, cpu-sleep-0-a76 on cpu6..cpu7) rises by about
one entry per injected period.

Assisted-by: Claude
---
--- a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/allwinner/sun60i-a733-cubie-a7s-minimal.dts
+++ b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/allwinner/sun60i-a733-cubie-a7s-minimal.dts
@@ -2543,6 +2543,120 @@
 	#cooling-cells = <2>;
 };
 
+/*
+ * Idle injection, the stage between throttling and the emergency shutdown.
+ *
+ * Without it the only actuator between the 90 C passive trip and the 110 C
+ * critical trip is cpufreq: if throttling to the lowest OPP cannot hold the
+ * part, the next event is a power-off. The stage itself is the vendor's: its
+ * sun60iw2p1.dtsi puts thermal-idle on cpu2..cpu7 and adds two idle zones
+ * with passive trips at 90 and 100 C, mapping cpu2-5 at 0..50 and cpu6-7 at
+ * 0..100 on the 100 C trip, and a vendor board registers idle-cpu2..7. The
+ * 100 C trip and the choice of cpu2..7 come from there. The map bounds do not:
+ * ours are lower 50 / upper unbounded (see below), the opposite of the vendor's
+ * upper limit of 50 on the A55s.
+ *
+ * Measured on a 6.6 board that already carries the mechanism: under a
+ * constant eight-core load, 50 % injection takes the CPU zones from 74-76 C to
+ * 64 C and lets both clusters return to full clock with cpufreq back at state 0;
+ * 100 % reaches 53-58 C. It removes real heat, and it costs roughly its own
+ * percentage in throughput -- which is why it sits ABOVE the cpufreq trip.
+ *
+ * It needs no hardware, and that is why it belongs in the default rather than
+ * beside the fan: the board with no cooling at all is exactly the one with
+ * nothing left at 100 C. Datasheet Ta max is 75 C with theta-JA 25.265 C/W, so a
+ * warm enclosure leaves very little room to begin with.
+ *
+ * Registration path, read rather than assumed: ARM_PSCI_CPUIDLE is =y in the
+ * arm64 defconfig, psci_idle_init_cpu() runs per CPU and each call ends in
+ * cpuidle_cooling_register(), which looks for a "thermal-idle" child of that
+ * CPU's node (drivers/thermal/cpuidle_cooling.c:249-260). One cooling device per
+ * CPU that carries the node, named idle-cpuN.
+ *
+ * cpu0 and cpu1 deliberately carry no node. Injecting idle on every core of the
+ * little cluster starves the system that is trying to recover; two cores stay
+ * uninjectable so userspace keeps running while the rest are parked.
+ *
+ * duration-us 10000: longer than the 3570 us min-residency of both core idle
+ * states, and also the worst case by which this stage can delay a softirq.
+ * exit-latency-us 500: the forced-idle path takes the deepest state whose
+ * entry + exit latency (dt_idle_states.c:64) fits the limit. 500 admits both
+ * core power-down states, cpu_sleep_0_a55 at 50 + 150 = 200 us on cpu2-5 and
+ * cpu_sleep_0_a76 at 50 + 210 = 260 us on cpu6-7, the slower one 240 us inside
+ * the limit, and it caps the late restart of the run window at 5 % of the 10 ms
+ * injected period. Against the same injection pinned to WFI, at a fixed 50 %
+ * under an eight-core load: both CPU zones ~1.8 C cooler and 0.21 W less at the
+ * wall, for the same injected time. See the commit message.
+ *
+ * Trip and map numbering: trip-2 and map1 are left free for the board fan
+ * patch (held back, not applied), so this takes trip-3 and map2 and the
+ * fan patch can drop in without renumbering. Reusing a node name would not
+ * create a second node -- dtc would merge the two and silently keep one
+ * temperature.
+ *
+ * The lower bound of 50 in the maps below is load-bearing, not decoration. A
+ * cpuidle cooling device is percentile with max_state fixed at 100
+ * (drivers/thermal/cpuidle_cooling.c). step_wise enters a newly throttled
+ * instance at clamp(cur_state + 1, lower, upper), so an unbounded lower reads
+ * as 0 and the stage starts at 1 % -- about 0.2 K of authority in a band 10 K
+ * wide -- then climbs one percent per poll and needs the trend to stay RAISING
+ * to do even that. Measured on a bare board: 100 C at t=70 s, 103 C at t=86 s,
+ * roughly thirty-two governor polls, injection states reached 4 and 1.
+ *
+ * 50 is the measured injection level above -- the one shown to remove the
+ * entire cpufreq throttling demand and buy ~10 K, which is exactly the width of
+ * the band between this trip and critical. It is applied on entry, before any
+ * ladder is traversed, so trend noise cannot keep the stage down. upper stays
+ * unbounded so it can still climb to 100 %.
+ */
+&cpu2 {
+	cpu2_idle: thermal-idle {
+		#cooling-cells = <2>;
+		duration-us = <10000>;
+		exit-latency-us = <500>;
+	};
+};
+
+&cpu3 {
+	cpu3_idle: thermal-idle {
+		#cooling-cells = <2>;
+		duration-us = <10000>;
+		exit-latency-us = <500>;
+	};
+};
+
+&cpu4 {
+	cpu4_idle: thermal-idle {
+		#cooling-cells = <2>;
+		duration-us = <10000>;
+		exit-latency-us = <500>;
+	};
+};
+
+&cpu5 {
+	cpu5_idle: thermal-idle {
+		#cooling-cells = <2>;
+		duration-us = <10000>;
+		exit-latency-us = <500>;
+	};
+};
+
+&cpu6 {
+	cpu6_idle: thermal-idle {
+		#cooling-cells = <2>;
+		duration-us = <10000>;
+		exit-latency-us = <500>;
+	};
+};
+
+&cpu7 {
+	cpu7_idle: thermal-idle {
+		#cooling-cells = <2>;
+		duration-us = <10000>;
+		exit-latency-us = <500>;
+	};
+};
+
 / {
 	thermal-zones {
 		cpul_thermal: cpul-thermal {
@@ -2562,6 +2676,11 @@
 					type = "critical";
 					hysteresis = <0>;
 				};
+				cpul_idle: cpul-trip-3 {
+					temperature = <100000>;
+					type = "passive";
+					hysteresis = <2000>;
+				};
 			};
 
 			cooling-maps {
@@ -2569,6 +2688,13 @@
 					trip = <&cpul_passive>;
 					cooling-device = <&cpu0 THERMAL_NO_LIMIT THERMAL_NO_LIMIT>;
 				};
+				map2 {
+					trip = <&cpul_idle>;
+					cooling-device = <&cpu2_idle 50 THERMAL_NO_LIMIT>,
+							<&cpu3_idle 50 THERMAL_NO_LIMIT>,
+							<&cpu4_idle 50 THERMAL_NO_LIMIT>,
+							<&cpu5_idle 50 THERMAL_NO_LIMIT>;
+				};
 			};
 		};
 
@@ -2589,6 +2715,11 @@
 					type = "critical";
 					hysteresis = <0>;
 				};
+				cpub_idle: cpub-trip-3 {
+					temperature = <100000>;
+					type = "passive";
+					hysteresis = <2000>;
+				};
 			};
 
 			cooling-maps {
@@ -2596,6 +2727,11 @@
 					trip = <&cpub_passive>;
 					cooling-device = <&cpu6 THERMAL_NO_LIMIT THERMAL_NO_LIMIT>;
 				};
+				map2 {
+					trip = <&cpub_idle>;
+					cooling-device = <&cpu6_idle 50 THERMAL_NO_LIMIT>,
+							<&cpu7_idle 50 THERMAL_NO_LIMIT>;
+				};
 			};
 		};
 
